{"task_id": "Mbpp/86", "solution": "class Validator:\n    \"\"\"Validates if an input is a positive integer.\"\"\"\n    def is_positive_integer(self, value) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the given value is an integer and greater than or equal to 1.\"\"\"\n        return isinstance(value, int) and value >= 1\n\n    def assert_valid(self, n: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Asserts that n is a positive integer, raising ValueError if invalid.\"\"\"\n        if not self.is_positive_integer(n):\n            raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer (n >= 1)\")\nclass CenteredHexagonalCalculator:\n    \"\"\"Calculates centered hexagonal numbers using a static formula.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def formula(n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the centered hexagonal number using the mathematical formula.\"\"\"\n        return 3 * n * (n - 1) + 1\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def compute(n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes the nth centered hexagonal number.\"\"\"\n        return CenteredHexagonalCalculator.formula(n)\ndef centered_hexagonal_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the nth centered hexagonal number.\n\n    Validates input n and calculates the number using a dedicated calculator.\n    \"\"\"\n    validator = Validator()\n    validator.assert_valid(n)\n\n    # Input(n) is used for traceability as per design, but not directly in calculation flow\n    # input_obj = Input(n)\n\n    # CenteredHexagonalCalculator uses static methods, so instantiation is not strictly necessary\n    # but the design's class diagram and sequence diagram show it.\n    calculator = CenteredHexagonalCalculator()\n    calculated_value = calculator.compute(n)\n\n    # Result(calculated_value) is used for traceability as per design\n    # result_obj = Result(calculated_value)\n\n    return calculated_value"}
